  "worked_solution": "The function $f(t)=u(t)g(t)$ is differentiable on the common domain where both $u(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable. The vector function $g(t)$ is differentiable on the common domain where its components are differentiable.  \n\nLet's now find the intervals where each of the functions $u(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable. \n\nFor the real-valued function $u(t):$  \n\n- We observe that $u(t)$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nFor the function $g(t):$ \n\n- The first component, $\\sqrt[3]{t-3}$, is differentiable over $(-\\infty ,3)\\cup (3,\\infty )$ as its derivative is not defined at $t=3.$\n\n- The second and third components, ${cos}^{2}⁡t$ and $\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}},$ are differentiable for all values of $t.$\n\nTherefore, the intersection of the domains of all three components of  $g(t)$ is $(-\\infty ,3)\\cup (3,\\infty ).$  \n\nFinally, the common domain of differentiability for $u(t)$ and $g(t)$ is $(-\\infty ,3)\\cup (3,\\infty ).$",
